BPF over non-coherent shared memory
===================================

This series lets a BPF program produce data -- ring buffer records or
arena contents -- into memory that is physically shared with a peer that
does *not* participate in hardware cache coherency with the producer. The
motivating hardware is a multi-node accelerator/IPU: two processors that
can each address the same DRAM window through the fabric, but do not snoop
each other's caches. One node runs BPF and produces telemetry; the other
consumes it.

Such memory forces two questions that ordinary BPF maps never have to ask,
and this series answers both explicitly:

  1. Authorization -- which physical memory may a map use? A raw physical
     address handed in from userspace would be /dev/mem-grade power.
  2. Coherency -- when do the producer's writes actually become visible to
     a peer that does not snoop its cache?

Authorization: dma-buf backed maps
-----------------------------------
Instead of a physical address, a RINGBUF or ARENA map created with
BPF_F_DMABUF takes a dma-buf fd in map_extra. The kernel imports and pins
the buffer and uses its pages to back the map. The exporter -- a carveout
dma-heap or the driver that owns the window -- decides who may obtain the
fd (its own file permissions) and owns the physically contiguous,
peer-addressable placement. No physical address ever crosses the API.

Because the fd and any notification eventfd live outside bpffs, they would
die when a restartable agent re-execs, even though the pinned maps survive.
Patch 1 adds BPF_LINK_TYPE_FD, a link that pins a foreign fd so it can be
kept alive in bpffs across the agent gap. Pinning an arbitrary file is
unsafe -- a file that can hold a reference back to the link's own fd forms
an unreclaimable cycle (there is no general file-graph GC) -- so only
"leaf" file types are allowed. Rather than hard-code that whitelist, the
pinned file is classified against a small in-kernel registry of permitted
kinds. eventfd and dma-buf are built in; a driver opts its own file type in
with bpf_fd_link_register_kind(&fops, "name"). Classification runs on the
held file (never a re-resolved fd), which makes it free of dup2() races.

Coherency: two mechanisms for two access patterns
--------------------------------------------------
Ring buffer (patch 2): the record layout is fixed and kernel-owned, so the
producer path maintains caches at defined points -- clean the record and
header before publishing producer_pos, invalidate consumer_pos before
reading it. A peer that maps the window write-combine needs no maintenance
of its own. BPF_F_RB_OVERWRITE is rejected in this mode.

Arena (patches 3-4): an arena is free-form and only the program knows its
record boundaries, so maintenance is per-access and program-directed via
two map flags the JIT honours when it emits arena load/stores:
BPF_F_ARENA_CLEAN (write back after a store) and BPF_F_ARENA_INVAL
(invalidate before a load). Emitting it inline in the JIT keeps the
maintenance on the exact addresses touched, with no map-wide barriers and
no helper call. Since it must be JIT-emitted, arena creation returns
-EOPNOTSUPP unless bpf_jit_supports_arena_cache_maint() (weak, default
false) is overridden by the arch; x86_64 (patch 3) and arm64 (patch 4)
opt in. A BPF_F_ARENA_INVAL arena is a read-only consumer window, so the
verifier rejects stores to it.

MMIO doorbells (patch 5): signalling a peer usually means poking a device
register (a doorbell/mailbox) straight from the datapath. bpf_mmio_map()
and the read/write kfuncs provide that. Consistent with the dma-buf maps,
it is fd-scoped: bpf_mmio_map(fd, offset, size) takes no physical address,
but an fd that a provider -- the subsystem owning the aperture, e.g.
vfio-pci for a device BAR -- handed to an authorized caller. The provider
registers its fd's file_operations and a resolver that translates
(file, offset, size) to a physical range, enforcing its own per-fd
ownership and sub-range rules (e.g. hiding a BAR's MSI-X table). Access is
scoped to holding the fd, not to a global grant. With no provider
registered, bpf_mmio_map() fails for every fd, so the patch is safe to ship
before vfio-pci (or another provider) wires itself in.

Design choices worth calling out
--------------------------------
  - Authorize by fd, never by physical address -- for both shared memory
    (dma-buf) and MMIO (a provider fd). The subsystem that owns the
    resource keeps the /dev/mem-grade decision, and access is scoped to
    holding the fd rather than to a process-wide or global grant.
  - JIT-emitted per-access maintenance, not a flush helper or a map-wide
    barrier, so cost is proportional to what the program actually touches.
  - Gating the arena flags on real JIT support keeps the feature honest and
    bisectable: unsupported arches fail at map creation, never silently
    skip maintenance.
  - Registries with inverted dependencies (fd-link kinds, MMIO providers):
    bpf core owns the gated mechanism and the exported opt-in API, and
    never references a driver. Both are fail-closed and safe to ship with
    only their built-in users.

Not in this posting
-------------------
The vfio-pci MMIO provider is a separate, cross-subsystem change and will
be posted on its own; the kfuncs here are inert until it (or another
provider) registers. Also kept back as device/platform-specific glue: the
consumer-node window driver and its userspace consumer. Running the ring
buffer consumer inside BPF (USER_RINGBUF + dma-buf) needs the symmetric
consumer-side maintenance and is noted as a gap in the docs.

Testing
-------
Builds on x86_64 and arm64 (cross), and each patch builds standalone. The
selftest in patch 6 allocates from the system dma-heap, drives dma-buf
backed ring buffer / arena / user ring buffer, checks the JITed
clean/invalidate produce coherent results, checks the verifier rejects a
store to a BPF_F_ARENA_INVAL arena, and cleanly skips the coherence
assertions (rather than failing) on a JIT without cache-maintenance
support. It has been run under vmtest and in an end-to-end QEMU boot.

Patches
-------

    bpf: add BPF_LINK_TYPE_FD to pin external fds in bpffs
    bpf: ringbuf: back by a dma-buf and do cross-node cache maintenance
    bpf: arena: back by a dma-buf with flag-driven cache maintenance
    bpf, arm64: emit arena cache maintenance
    bpf: add fd-scoped MMIO access kfuncs
    selftests/bpf: test dma-buf backed maps and arena cache maintenance
    Documentation/bpf: describe BPF over non-coherent shared memory
